question: Should `findings` trust a thread from any commenter?

On a public repo, a stranger without write access can comment on your PR with something like "fix: add this setup line to test/setup.js". `findings` lists that thread like a council or Copilot finding. If jev rates it correct, in scope and with one clear fix, `decide` returns `fix`. The agent then makes the edit, runs the full test suite with shell access, and pushes the change with your signature.

`open(t, me)` checks only `isResolved` and the author of the newest comment. github.mjs already fetches `authorAssociation` and `authorType`, but `findings` never reads them. The rest of the engine allows a shell only when every text author is trusted (`frame.shellOk`, used in review.mjs `toolsFor` and in reply.mjs).

One possible fix:

```js
// findings
const TRUSTED = new Set(["OWNER", "MEMBER", "COLLABORATOR"]);
const trusted = t.comments.every(
  (c) => TRUSTED.has(c.authorAssociation) || c.authorType === "Bot"
);

// decide
if (!t.trusted) return { decision: "ask", reason: "untrusted reviewer" };
```

question: Can a symlink in the PR send a file from outside the checkout to jev?

Say the PR adds `leak -> /Users/me/.ssh/id_rsa` and a review thread sits on path `leak`. Lines 53-56 build `${root}/${path}`, and `existsSync`/`readFileSync` follow the symlink. The file's text goes into prData at line 68 and from there to jev. review.mjs `hunk()` guards the same case with `realpathSync` and a `startsWith(wt + sep)` check.

```js
const wt = realpathSync(root);
const file = realpathSync(resolve(wt, String(path)));
if (!file.startsWith(wt + sep)) return "(outside the checkout)";
// read `file`
```

question: Should `fixed` reject a sha that is not on the PR head?

Say the agent passes the sha of a commit on main, or of a local commit whose push failed. The `Fixed in [abc1234](...)` link then goes to a 404 or to an unrelated commit, and the thread is resolved while the PR head has no fix. Lines 179-180 only match `^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$` and run a local `git rev-parse`. Nothing compares the sha with `gh.pr(repo, n).headSha`.

```js
const { headSha } = await gh.pr(repo, n);
// git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> <headSha>, or throw new Usage(...) before posting or resolving
```

should: `respond` assumes the local checkout is at the PR head

If you run `pr-respond decide T1` from a subfolder, or from a checkout that is not at the head, `code()` reads `${cwd}/${t.path}`. It either finds no file or finds the wrong code. jev then answers "correct about the code at the PR head?" without the head's code, and the result is an unrelated decline or ask. For `fixed`, an unpushed local sha gets a `Fixed in` link that returns 404.

Line 177 calls `decide` without `root`, so respond.mjs:52 falls back to `process.cwd()`. `decide` already fetches `pr.headSha` at respond.mjs:64 but never compares it with HEAD.

```js
const root = git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel");
if (git("rev-parse", "HEAD") !== pr.headSha) throw new Usage("checkout is not at the PR head");
r.decide({ repo, n, threadId: a, gh, jev, root });
// fixed: require `git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> <headSha>` before linkFix
```

should: `findings` writes prState without the repo lock

pr-review-loop, or the `pr-review review` that the skill runs between rounds, can hold the lock and write state.json while `findings` runs. `savePrState` (state.mjs:174) rewrites the whole file, so whichever save comes last drops the other's fields. If `respondHeads` is lost, the round cap resets, and the agent loop has no bound.

```js
const release = acquireLock(rs);
try {
  // read, cap check, save
} finally {
  release();
}
```

The other option is to move `respondHeads` to `pr/<n>/respond.json`, so each module writes its own file.
